digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56732-x
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to develop and evaluate a global, phylogenetically informed meta-analysis of pesticide effects, testing whether insecticides, fungicides and herbicides impair growth, reproduction, behaviour and metabolic biomarkers of non-target organisms across major taxa, exposure pathways and pesticide classes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When pesticide impacts are widespread, globally consequential, poorly synthesized across taxa and settings, and measurable across organism groups, response categories and exposure pathways, a global meta-analysis is a justified research aim."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) If global pesticide consequences remain poorly understood without unified synthesis, then knowledge from a careful quantitative synthesis is vital for informing national and international policy goals."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The included evidence base spans studies measuring effects of insecticides, fungicides and herbicides on animals, plants and microorganisms, within broad experimental, climatic and aquatic/terrestrial settings."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The meta-analytic classifications cover multicellular animals, plants and microorganisms and classify response outcomes including growth, reproduction, animal behaviour and other relevant biomarkers."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The study addresses both taxa-specific pesticide consequences and potential exposure pathways by which pesticides can affect non-target organisms."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Within this broad non-target-effects framework, the study also assesses whether newer and older pesticides differ in their effects."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The unresolved global consequences of pesticide use, the need for a robust quantitative evidence base, and the broad study scope across pesticide classes, major organism groups, response categories and exposure pathways motivate a global quantitative meta-analysis of non-target pesticide effects."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The continuing debate over real-world pesticide hazards can be explained by recognized concerns about widespread non-target impacts, trophic-level propagation, and limited regulatory assessments."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because global pesticide consequences across diverse ecosystems and taxa remain poorly understood without a unified synthesis, an analysis is needed to support a robust evidence base for non-target effects."];
  "c1" [label="(3,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from s1 and induction-common from i1, therefore some of the negative impacts of pesticides are known."];
  "c2" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from s2 and induction-common from c1, therefore negative impacts on non-target organisms are established and some are known."];
  "c3" [label="(5,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from s4 and induction-common from c2, therefore such impacts appear widespread across taxa, with individual studies reporting effects on microorganisms, plants, invertebrates, amphibians, birds and mammals."];
  "c4" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given induction-case from s6 and induction-common from c3, therefore the impacts of pesticides are widespread across taxa and can propagate across trophic levels."];
  "c8" [label="(13,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given abduction-phenomenon from s12 and abduction-knowledge from i5, therefore to our knowledge, there has been no systematic and overarching synthesis of how different types of pesticides affect the diversity of multiple non-target eukaryotic and prokaryotic organisms across all trophic levels."];
  "c9" [label="(15,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given abduction-phenomenon from s14 and abduction-knowledge from c8, therefore without a unified synthesis applying common data capture and analytical standards, the global consequences of pesticide use across diverse ecosystems and taxa remain poorly understood."];
  "i1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: In agricultural science, global use of pesticides is known to have potential for non-target effects based on domain consensus."];
  "i3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Widespread non-target impacts, trophic propagation, and limited regulatory assessments are known to cause significant debate among agricultural industry, governmental bodies and conservation organizations concerning real-world hazards posed by pesticides."];
  "i4" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If a major factor is the lack of cross-taxa synthesis, then such an analysis is needed to support a robust evidence base for non-target effects that extend beyond scenario-specific case studies and model species."];
  "i5" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If previous meta-analyses are limited to particular taxonomic groups or habitats, then there has been no systematic and overarching synthesis of how different types of pesticides affect the diversity of multiple non-target eukaryotic and prokaryotic organisms across all trophic levels."];
  "i6" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If the global consequences remain poorly understood without unified synthesis, then knowledge derived from a careful quantitative synthesis is vital to inform national and international policy goals to provide a framework for targeted mitigation to identify the role of pesticide use in biodiversity declines."];
  "i7" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: The included studies encompass laboratory and field experiments from temperate and tropical climatic zones, both in aquatic and terrestrial systems, measuring effects of insecticides, fungicides and herbicides on animals, plants and microorganisms."];
  "s1" [label="(1,0,0) Induction-case: Pesticides are used globally to support agricultural production and protect human and animal health in both domestic and commercial situations."];
  "s12" [label="(12,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Previous meta-analyses of pesticide impacts on non-target species have either considered only particular taxonomic groups, such as fish or bees, or have considered specific habitats such as aquatic ecosystems."];
  "s14" [label="(14,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Furthermore, current syntheses have not considered how the impacts of pesticides differ globally across climatic zones or for major mechanisms of exposure, such as those acting in aquatic or terrestrial environments."];
  "s18" [label="(18,0,0) Induction-case: These studies collectively measured the effects of insecticides, fungicides and herbicides on animals (invertebrates and vertebrates), plants (dicotyledonous, monocotyledonous and spore-producing) and microorganisms (bacteria and fungi)."];
  "s2" [label="(2,0,0) Induction-case: At sufficient environmental concentrations, insecticide, fungicide and herbicide exposure can affect non-target organisms by disrupting their survival, growth, reproduction and behaviour, as well as effects on other metabolic and physiological processes."];
  "s20" [label="(20,0,0) Induction-case: We classified the effects on multicellular animals (vertebrates and invertebrates), plants and microorganisms based on changes in growth, reproduction, and behaviour (animals only) as well as other relevant biomarkers (e.g. enzymatic activities, metabolic reaction and plant photosynthesis)."];
  "s22" [label="(22,0,0) Induction-case: As well as considering the consequences of taxa-specific pesticides (e.g. effect of herbicides on plants), we also address potential pathways by which pesticides can affect non-target organisms (including cross-class impacts e.g. insecticides affecting fungi or plants)."];
  "s24" [label="(24,0,0) Induction-case: Further, we assess whether ‘new’ or ‘old’ pesticides, based on the rigorous European Union (EU) regulatory framework, differ in their effects."];
  "s4" [label="(4,0,0) Induction-case: For example, fungicides may decrease the biomass of arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi affecting symbioses with higher plants; herbicides may reduce plant pollen viability and carbohydrate metabolism; insecticides targeting pest herbivores may cause long-term declines in non-target insect pollinators associated with mass-flowering crops (incorporating reference viewpoints on specific taxa impacts)."];
  "s6" [label="(6,0,0) Induction-case: In addition to effects on individual species, the influence of pesticides can propagate across trophic levels, impacting ecosystem-scale species interactions that may lead to secondary effects."];
  "s9" [label="(9,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: There remains significant debate among the agricultural industry, governmental bodies and conservation organizations concerning the real-world hazards posed by pesticides."];
  "s1" -> "c1" [label="induction-case"];
  "i1" -> "c1" [label="induction-common"];
